public static List<String> topKFrequent(String[] words, int k) {
    Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (String word : words) {
        freq.put(word, freq.getOrDefault(word, 0) + 1);
    }

    PriorityQueue<String> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
        (a, b) -> {
            if (freq.get(a).equals(freq.get(b))) {
                return b.compareTo(a);
            }
            return freq.get(a) - freq.get(b);
        }
    );

    for (String word : freq.keySet()) {
        heap.offer(word);
        if (heap.size() > k) {
            heap.poll();
        }
    }

    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
        result.add(heap.poll());
    }
    Collections.reverse(result);
    return result;
}



1) High‑level purpose: “At a high level, this function returns the k most frequent words, sorted by descending frequency 
and lexicographical order for ties.”

2) Inputs & outputs: “The input is an array of strings and an integer k.
The output is a list of k words ordered by descending frequency, and lexicographically for ties.”

3) Comparator logic (most important part): “This comparator creates a min‑heap where words with lower frequency have higher 
priority.If frequencies are equal, the lexicographically larger word is prioritized so that it gets removed first when the 
heap exceeds size k.”

4) Why reverse at the end?: “Since elements are extracted from the min‑heap in ascending priority order, reversing the list 
ensures the final result is in descending frequency order.”

5) Time & space complexity:
Time complexity:
Building frequency map: O(n)
Heap operations: O(n log k)
Total: O(n log k)
Space complexity: Frequency map + heap: O(n + k)

6)  Edge case / pitfall:
Examples you could say:
k > number of unique words
k == 0
Empty input array
Case sensitivity assumptions
✅ Model answer:
“If k is larger than the number of unique words, the function returns all unique words.”


